Granulomatosis with polyangiitis
Rheumatology & Immunology
Saddle-nose deformity, Cavitary lung nodules, c-ANCA/anti-PR3 positivity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-020
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of coughing up blood, combined with severe, painful facial swelling around the eye, and worsening of chronic bloody nasal discharge, requiring immediate evaluation to rule out acute pulmonary hemorrhage and orbital compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient with a chronic destructive nasal condition now presenting with new-onset hemoptysis and painful visual changes. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was critical to evaluate airway safety, assess for imminent visual loss from an orbital mass, and facilitate immediate hospital transfer to halt progressive systemic necrotizing vasculitis, as delaying until standard hours risked irreversible tissue destruction or asphyxiation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I'm coughing up dark blood, my left eye is bulging and incredibly painful, and my nose won't stop bleeding."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 6-month history of chronic, purulent, and bloody nasal discharge that has been completely resistant to multiple courses of conventional oral antibiotics. Over this time, the patient has developed a noticeable collapse of the nasal bridge. Today, the patient experienced the sudden onset of recurrent hemoptysis and a severe, chronic cough. Concurrently, the left eye has become acutely painful, swollen, and feels like it is pushing outward (proptosis), causing double vision (diplopia). The patient also complains of profound fatigue, migratory polyarthralgias affecting the knees and wrists, and feeling generally unwell. Reports their urine has been dark red today.
Functional Status: Resting in a chair; severely distressed by the eye pain and coughing up blood.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of visual loss and pulmonary hemorrhage; spouse is highly anxious.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 150/90 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient appears chronically ill, acutely distressed, holding a blood-stained tissue, with obvious facial asymmetry.
Physical Exam: HEENT: Gross nasal septal perforation visible with a characteristic saddle-nose deformity (collapse of the nasal bridge). The left eye exhibits significant proptosis (bulging outward), periorbital edema, and conjunctival injection. Extraocular movements are restricted, causing diplopia, strongly suggesting an orbital pseudotumor (granulomatous mass). Respiratory: Mildly tachypneic. Auscultation reveals focal crackles in the right upper zone, raising suspicion for fleeting, cavitary pulmonary infiltrates or nodules. Cardiovascular: Tachycardic. Dermatological: Scattered palpable purpura noted on the lower extremities. Renal: Patient confirms acute hematuria, raising high suspicion for necrotizing glomerulonephritis with red blood cell casts. Diagnostic tetrad completely aligns clinically: destructive saddle-nose deformity, high suspicion for cavitary lung nodules, anticipating positive c-ANCA/anti-PR3 and necrotizing granulomatous inflammation on hospital biopsy.
Environmental Observations: Tissues stained with dark blood on the table.
Medication Reconciliation: Multiple empty packets of over-the-counter decongestants and previous antibiotics.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Granulomatosis with polyangiitis - Status: Exacerbated (Systemic vasculitic crisis with orbital/pulmonary involvement)
Differentials:

Severe invasive bacterial sinusitis with orbital cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The systemic multi-organ involvement (hemoptysis, purpura, hematuria) and the chronic, indolent, destructive nature of the nasal deformity prior to this acute flare.

Goodpasture Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prominent, severe, and destructive upper respiratory tract features (saddle-nose, orbital mass) which are classic for GPA and absent in Goodpasture's.

Tuberculosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of the orbital pseudotumor, the classic saddle-nose deformity, and the acute renal involvement (hematuria), which points definitively to an autoimmune vasculitis rather than an infectious granulomatous disease.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Threat to vision and airway)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is presenting in an acute, multi-system crisis of a severe necrotizing vasculitis, characterized by pulmonary hemorrhage, acute glomerulonephritis, and a sight-threatening orbital granuloma. The combination of hemoptysis, dark urine, and a painful, bulging eye constitutes a critical medical emergency. An urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely vital to physically confirm the extent of the orbital and pulmonary compromise, distinguish the presentation from a simple sinusitis flare, and mandate immediate emergency hospital transfer. Leaving this patient unassessed posed immediate risks of permanent blindness, renal failure, or fatal pulmonary hemorrhage, unequivocally meeting the MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The patient requires immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via ambulance. The hospital will need to perform urgent CT imaging of the orbits, sinuses, and chest, urgent renal function testing and urinalysis, ANCA serology, and initiate immediate high-dose intravenous pulse corticosteroids (and likely cyclophosphamide or rituximab) to halt the necrotizing tissue destruction.
Positive Behavioral Change: Eye protection anchoring: Apply a cool, damp washcloth lightly over the closed left eye while waiting; utilizing this physical barrier immediately reduces the painful sensation of exposure and provides mild symptomatic relief from the orbital pressure. Strict upright positioning: Remain sitting upright at a 45-degree angle; proving that this position physically prevents the blood in the airway from pooling in the lower lungs, actively assisting in maintaining oxygenation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None; critical care admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance transfer organized. If the patient begins to cough up massive volumes of bright red blood, loses consciousness, or if the vision in the left eye suddenly goes completely black, update the 000 dispatcher immediately to upgrade the priority.

